> **To write:** the opening, in a paragraph or two that start from the sentence above. A stranger
> reads it to decide whether this is for them, so say, with every claim checked against the code
> and every value, such as an interval, a port or a path, copied from it rather than from memory:
>
{%- if primary == 'library' %}
> - the problem it solves, and for whom;
> - how it differs from the crates people use for that now, named, or by kind when you can't
>   vouch for the comparison;
> - its limits: what it leaves to the caller, and what it never does.
{%- else %}
> - if it's mainly one owner's configuration that a program acts on, say so, since its honest Quick
>   start is then how a change is made here;
> - who it's for, and what it does for them, in their words rather than the code's;
> - how it differs from what they'd use instead, named, or by kind when you can't vouch for the
>   comparison;
{%- if primary == 'terminal' or primary == 'desktop' %}
> - whether it runs beside something else, such as a game or an editor, and whether it reads or
>   changes another program;
{%- endif %}
> - its limits: what it doesn't do, and what it never sends, stores or {{ 'logs' if primary == 'service' else 'shows' }};
> - if it spends money, such as on paid API calls, say what on, and whether the Quick start can
>   spend it by accident;
> - what it contacts, and what it does, with no key, credentials or login.
{%- endif %}

