## Quick start

{% if primary == 'library' -%}
> **To write:** the shortest path from nothing to something the reader can see work, as numbered
> steps with commands to copy, starting with what the reader needs. Depth, such as every option or
> platform, goes on a page under `docs/`, with an entry under Documentation. When what the reader
> needs, or where its files live and how to remove it, runs past a few lines, give it a section of
> its own between Quick start and Documentation, such as `## Requirements`, and add it to
> `readme_sections` in `Cargo.toml`.
{%- else -%}
> **To write:** the shortest path from nothing to something the reader can see work, as numbered
> steps with commands to copy, starting with what the reader needs, every tool the commands call
> included, such as curl or jq. Say how to remove it, after the last step or in its own section.
> Depth, such as every option or platform, goes on a page under `docs/`, with an entry under
> Documentation. When what the reader needs, or where its files live and how to remove it, runs past
> a few lines, give it a section of its own between Quick start and Documentation, such as
> `## Requirements`, and add it to `readme_sections` in `Cargo.toml`.
{%- endif %}
> For this {{ label[primary] }}, cover:
>
{%- if primary == 'web' %}
> - where it listens, and how a stranger reaches the UI: the bind address, a proxy or tunnel, and
>   any feature that needs HTTPS;
> - how the first account is made, if it has accounts;
> - the outside systems its pages need;
> - if it has a deploy kit, whether it suits any host, or holds steps that only fit the owner's;
{%- elif primary == 'desktop' %}
> - the supported OS versions, and what each needs before it runs, such as a system webview, GTK
>   and WebKitGTK, or a keychain service;
> - the install form, such as an installer, a portable zip or a dmg, and whether it installs per
>   user or needs an administrator;
> - whether it's signed, and if not, the warning the OS shows, such as SmartScreen or Gatekeeper,
>   in the words on the screen;
> - where its files live on each OS, and what uninstalling leaves;
> - how it updates, and how it proves an update genuine;
{%- elif primary == 'terminal' %}
> - the system and the shell the commands are for;
> - the minimum terminal size, and the terminals it's tested in;
> - where its window goes if it runs beside something else;
> - outside accounts or profiles its data depends on;
> - a demo or offline mode, if it has one, as the first check that it runs;
> - whether it's signed, and the warning a user sees if not;
> - whether it updates itself;
> - the keys that matter in the first minute;
{%- elif primary == 'cli' %}
> - the install forms, and the shell the commands are for;
> - what it needs first, such as a token or a config file, and where that lives;
> - whether it's signed, and the warning a user sees if not;
> - output for scripts, such as exit codes or JSON, if a page doesn't cover it;
{%- elif primary == 'service' %}
> - how it's reached, and how a reader consumes what it produces: its port, its authentication
>   and the credentials each install path has, and any proxy in front;
> - the configuration that matters: the environment variables, where its data persists, and how
>   much that grows per day;
> - the long-running install, such as a deploy kit or a systemd unit, if it isn't the Quick start,
>   and what that host needs, such as memory and disk;
> - the outside services it calls: their keys and quotas, and the load it puts on them;
> - what a restart or a reboot does, what it misses while it's down, and how it relates to other
>   copies of it, such as a secondary;
{%- else %}
> - the dependency, which trellis filled in below: keep it as the first step;
> - the minimum Rust version, which `rust-version` in `Cargo.toml` sets, and any `no_std` or
>   async runtime needs;
> - a whole program that calls it, if the example above isn't one;
{%- endif %}
{%- for other in others %}
> - its {{ label[other] }}: {{ {'web': 'where its UI listens, how to reach it, and the page the reader sees first;', 'desktop': 'how to start it, and its first window;', 'terminal': 'how to start it, and its first screen;', 'cli': 'the command a person runs first, and what it prints, as a step;', 'service': 'how to start it in the background, and how to check that it runs;'}[other] }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- if primary in ['web', 'service'] %}
> - how a new user gets a database or other state the program won't create itself, with any
>   container image named in full, such as `docker.io/library/postgres`, which stock Podman needs;
{%- elif primary != 'library' %}
> - how a new user gets a database or other state the program won't create itself;
{%- endif %}
{%- if primary != 'library' and visibility == 'private' %}
> - if a step clones the repository, the credentials that needs while it's private;
{%- endif %}
{%- if primary == 'desktop' %}
> - the last step: the first window, and what the reader sees in it, with and without a
>   connection, and any permissions or sign-ins it asks for.
{%- else %}
> - the last step: {{ {'web': 'open the page, and what the reader sees there.', 'terminal': 'the first screen, and what the reader sees on it.', 'cli': 'the first command, and what it prints.', 'service': 'a health check, or a request and its response.', 'library': 'run the program, and what it prints.'}[primary] }}
{%- endif %}
